Below is a minimal, self‑contained Java snippet that shows how to use the **`NodeIdConverter.format`** helper to append the node‑ID (if it exists) to a `StringBuilder` that you’re building up for a log entry.

```java
import org.elasticsearch.logging.log4j.LogEvent;          // or whatever LogEvent type you use
import org.elasticsearch.logging.log4j.NodeIdConverter;   // the helper that does the work

public class LogExample {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // 1. Create the StringBuilder that will hold the final log line
        StringBuilder logBuilder = new StringBuilder();

        // 2. Build the rest of the log message first (timestamp, level, etc.)
        logBuilder.append("[INFO] ")
                  .append("Processing request ... ");

        // 3. Create or obtain the LogEvent that contains the node‑ID
        LogEvent logEvent = createLogEvent();   // <-- replace with your real event

        // 4. Append the node‑ID (if present) to the StringBuilder
        //    The helper mutates the StringBuilder that you pass in.
        NodeIdConverter.format(logEvent, logBuilder);

        // 5. Finish the log line (e.g., add a newline)
        logBuilder.append(System.lineSeparator());

        // 6. Output the final log line
        System.out.print(logBuilder.toString());
    }

    // Dummy method – replace with your real LogEvent creation logic
    private static LogEvent createLogEvent() {
        // In a real scenario this would be supplied by Log4j/Elasticsearch
        return new LogEvent() {
            @Override public String getMessage() { return "dummy"; }
            @Override public String getLoggerName() { return "my.logger"; }
            @Override public String getThreadName() { return "main"; }
            @Override public long getTimeMillis() { return System.currentTimeMillis(); }
            @Override public String getLevel() { return "INFO"; }
            @Override public String getNodeId() { return "node-1234"; } // <-- node ID
            // … implement other methods as needed
        };
    }
}
```

### What the helper does

* `NodeIdConverter.format(LogEvent event, StringBuilder sb)`  
  * Checks whether the `LogEvent` contains a non‑null node‑ID (`event.getNodeId()`).
  * If a node‑ID is present, it appends it to the supplied `StringBuilder` in the format you want (by default it just appends the raw ID, but you can customize the format inside the helper if needed).

### Why this works

* The `StringBuilder` is passed by reference, so the helper mutates it directly—no need to re‑assign the result.
* The helper is part of Elasticsearch’s internal logging utilities, so it already knows how to extract the node‑ID from the event object.

Feel free to adapt the snippet to your own logging framework or to add additional context (e.g., timestamps, thread names, etc.) before or after the node‑ID.

